insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-lowest-evaluated-cost-over-the-life', 1, 'On the materials tender, MS5 quotes a total of 487200.000000, the lowest of the five quoted totals, and scores a technical percentage of 50.000000 against a pass mark of 60. What happens to its price in a lowest-cost award?', '["MS5 becomes the lowest evaluated cost, since the award now rests on price and the technical score falls away", "Its commercial envelope is never opened, so its price takes no part in ranking the evaluated costs", "Opened and then rejected at the commercial stage for failing the pass mark by 10 points", "It enters the ranking with a penalty equal to its shortfall below the pass mark, added to its price"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine returns MS5 with the reason "technical score 50 is below the pass mark 60; the commercial envelope is not opened". A lowest-cost award still runs the technical envelope first, so MS5''s quoted total never reaches the commercial stage. No penalty is added and no commercial rejection happens, because nothing is opened. The lowest evaluated cost is MS4.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-lowest-evaluated-cost-over-the-life', 2, 'Which texts does the engine cite for an award that goes to the lowest evaluated cost with no rated criteria?', '["World Bank Regulations (7th ed.) para 5.50, the weighting matrix, with s.14 of the Nigerian Oil and Gas Industry Content Development Act 2010", "The Standard Procurement Document ITB 34.1 alone, because the award is made on price after any omission is priced", "Annex X paras 3.7 and 3.8 only, since every award without rated criteria must include a life-cycle cost", "World Bank Procurement Regulations (7th ed.) para 5.70 and the Public Procurement Act 2007 s.24(3) and s.33(1)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s award source for a lowest-cost award reads "World Bank Procurement Regulations (7th ed.) para 5.70; Nigeria Public Procurement Act 2007 s.24(3) and s.33(1)". Para 5.50 sets the Rated Criteria band for a combined award. ITB 34.1 prices an omission and Annex X paras 3.7 and 3.8 support the life-cycle cost; both are terms inside the evaluated cost, and neither is the award rule.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-lowest-evaluated-cost-over-the-life', 3, 'MS3 scores the highest technical percentage of the materials bids, 90.000000. Under the materials tender''s lowest-cost award, what does that score earn MS3?', '["A pass through the technical envelope and nothing more; the award ranks passing bids on evaluated cost alone", "A technical weight of 0.7 on its score, which places it first on combined score", "Its commercial score, which is scaled up by 90 divided by the highest technical percentage", "The award itself, because it clears the pass mark by the widest margin among the four"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With no rated criteria the passing bids are ranked on evaluated cost alone, and no technical weight enters the award. MS3''s evaluated cost, 581453.933847, is the highest of the four responsive bids, so it ranks fourth. A technical weight of 0.7 and a relative technical score belong to the combined award of the well services tender.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-lowest-evaluated-cost-over-the-life', 4, 'The engine''s basis for the materials life cycle reads "net present cost of 5 years of annual costs at 0.1 a year, end-of-year discounting, residual value credited in the last year". Where does the discounting itself come from?', '["A discounting routine written inside tender.js, apart from the economics engines", "The lib/stats library, which also carries the seeded mulberry32 stream the engine samples with", "The canonical npv function of engines/economics/cashflow.ts, which the tender engine imports", "Annex X of the World Bank Regulations, typed into the engine as a discount table"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis ends "through engines/economics/cashflow.ts npv", and the engine''s imports list economics/cashflow.ts for exactly that. It carries no Monte Carlo or NPV code of its own. lib/stats is imported for sampling, and Annex X paras 3.7 and 3.8 are the citation for counting life-cycle costs, with no discount table in them.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-lowest-evaluated-cost-over-the-life', 5, 'At 0.1 a year and end-of-year discounting, MS4''s valve maintenance of 7000.000000 in year 1 discounts to 6363.636364. What does the same 7000.000000 paid in year 5 count for?', '["6363.636364, as every year is discounted from the end of year 1", "4346.449261, discounted by the year 5 factor 0.620921", "7000.000000 in full, since the last year of a life cycle is left undiscounted by the engine", "5259.203606, which is the discounted figure for the middle year of the five"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each year''s cost is discounted from the end of its own year by 1 / (1 + 0.1) raised to the year. Year 5''s factor is 0.620921, and 7000.000000 discounts to 4346.449261. 6363.636364 is year 1 and 5259.203606 is year 3. No year is left undiscounted; the five discounted figures sum to 26535.507386.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-lowest-evaluated-cost-over-the-life', 6, 'MS4''s corrected price is 503930.000000 and its life-cycle cost 26535.507386. How does the engine treat the award price when it builds the life-cycle term?', '["The price is discounted at 0.1 over the five years along with the valve maintenance of each year", "Spread evenly over the five years, with each year''s share of the price discounted", "It discounts the price at year 1 only, so a sum paid at the award counts for less than its face", "It leaves the price undiscounted, because the price is paid at the start"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course states it plainly: the engine does not discount the award price, which is paid at the start, and only the annual costs of years 1 to 5 are discounted. The life-cycle cost of 26535.507386 is the five discounted maintenance years and nothing else. Spreading or discounting the price would build a different figure from the one the engine returns.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-lowest-evaluated-cost-over-the-life', 7, 'A materials bid gives four annual costs while the life cycle states 5 years. What does the engine return?', '["Four years of life-cycle cost, with the missing fifth year counted as a cost of 0", "One result for that bid, with the reason that year 5 was filled with the mean of the other four", "A refusal: \"bids[0].annualCosts must be an array of 5 finite numbers, one per life-cycle year\"", "A refusal that names lifeCycle.years, since the years then have to be cut to 4 to match the bid"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine refuses the call by the field it names, bids[0].annualCosts, with the message quoted in the key. It never pads the missing year: a zero for a year the bidder never priced would lower that bid''s evaluated cost. lifeCycle.years is the field refused for a life cycle of 0 years, whose message reads "lifeCycle.years must be a whole number from 1 to 100".'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-lowest-evaluated-cost-over-the-life', 8, 'Two stated bids R1 and R2 are identical (life cycle 3 years at 0.08, annual costs 1500 a year) except that R1 states a residual value of 4000. The engine gives R1 a life-cycle cost of 690.316517 and R2 3865.645481. Where does the difference of 3175.328964 come from?', '["The 4000 is taken off year 3''s cost before discounting, so it is 4000 / 1.08^3", "4000 is credited in full at the award date, less the year 1 factor", "A credit of 4000 is spread over the three years, a third of it in each year before discounting", "It is subtracted once the life-cycle cost is summed, less 0.08 of it"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine credits the residual value in the last year: "residual value credited in the last year". The difference, 3175.328964, is checked to be 4000 / 1.08^3. Crediting it at the award date would be worth the full 4000, and spreading it over three years discounts parts of it at larger factors; neither is the rule the engine states.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-lowest-evaluated-cost-over-the-life', 9, 'What does the engine return for a bid whose residualValue is given as text?', '["The life-cycle cost with the residual value ignored and a note that the text could not be read", "Zero residual value in the last year, along with the reason that the text was dropped", "A refusal naming lifeCycle, as the residual value is part of the life-cycle settings of the call", "A refusal: \"bids[0].residualValue must be a finite number when given\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine refuses by the field it names, bids[0].residualValue, with the message in the key. It never drops a term silently, and it does not name lifeCycle: the residual value belongs to the bid, and lifeCycle carries only the years and the discount rate.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-lowest-evaluated-cost-over-the-life', 10, 'On the fixture settings, MS4''s evaluated cost is 546244.982386. What does the same call return for MS4 with lifeCycle left out?', '["503930.000000, as the evaluated cost falls back to the corrected price", "519709.475000, which is the corrected price with the omission and the schedule adjustment", "546244.982386 again, as annual costs count with or without a life cycle", "527014.250000, the evaluated cost MS2 has on the materials tender when the life cycle is left out"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Without a life cycle MS4''s evaluated cost is 519709.475000: the corrected price 503930.000000 plus the omission 12000.000000 and the schedule adjustment 3779.475000. The life cycle adds 26535.507386 to reach 546244.982386. 527014.250000 is MS2''s figure without the life cycle, and 503930.000000 leaves out the omission and the schedule term, which do not depend on a life cycle.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-lowest-evaluated-cost-over-the-life', 11, 'With and without the life cycle, the four responsive materials bids rank MS4, MS2, MS1, MS3 both ways. What does the life cycle do to the gap between MS4 and MS2?', '["Widens it from 1618.594846 to 7304.775000, as MS4''s maintenance is the dearest", "Nothing: the gap stays at 7304.775000, since the order of the bids does not change", "It narrows it from 7304.775000 to 1618.594846", "It reverses the order of the pair, so MS2 now leads MS4 by 1618.594846 overall"]'::jsonb, 2, 'MS4 carries the dearest maintenance, 7000.000000 a year, against MS2''s 5500.000000, so the life cycle adds more to MS4 (26535.507386) than to MS2 (20849.327232). The gap falls from 7304.775000 to 1618.594846, and the order is checked to be the same both ways. Nothing reverses; the order holding does not mean the gap holds.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-lowest-evaluated-cost-over-the-life', 12, 'MS4 omits the inspection line. MS2, MS1 and MS3 price it at 9500.000000, 12000.000000 and 14500.000000, and MS5, which failed the pass mark, also quotes one. What does the engine add to MS4 under the average rule?', '["12000.000000, the average of the three responsive bids that price it", "The average over all four bids that quote the line, MS5 included, as every quoted price counts", "9500.000000, the lowest of the three responsive prices, so the omitting bid is given the benefit", "Nothing, as an omitted item stays out until the bidder prices it"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under World Bank SPD ITB 34.1 an omitted item is priced at the average of the corrected amounts quoted by the other responsive bids: MS2, MS1 and MS3 give 12000.000000. MS5''s price envelope is never opened, so its figure is not in the average. The engine refuses ''lowest'' as an omission rule by name, and an omission is always priced.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-lowest-evaluated-cost-over-the-life', 13, 'MS4 delivers in 11 weeks on a schedule of minWeeks 8 at ratePerWeek 0.0025, with no discount. What schedule adjustment does the engine add to MS4?', '["1314.250000, the adjustment the engine adds to MS2, which delivers in 9 weeks", "2701.500000, as each week beyond 8 is charged on the average price of the bids", "Nothing, since 11 weeks sits inside the window of 8 to 14 weeks", "3779.475000, for the 3 weeks beyond the minimum at 0.0025 of its corrected price"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The adjustment is ratePerWeek x the weeks beyond minWeeks x (corrected price less the discount). MS4 is 3 weeks beyond 8, and the engine adds 3779.475000. A delivery inside the window is still adjusted for every week past the minimum; only beyond 14 weeks is a bid rejected. 1314.250000 is MS2''s term and 2701.500000 is MS1''s.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-lowest-evaluated-cost-over-the-life', 14, 'Which materials bid has the smallest life-cycle cost, and what does it pay for its delivery schedule?', '["MS4, at 26535.507386, since it offers the lowest corrected price of the four responsive bids", "MS3, at 18953.933847, with no schedule adjustment as it delivers at the minimum of 8 weeks", "MS2, at 20849.327232, and it also earns a credit of 1314.250000 for its early delivery", "MS1, at 22744.720616, with a schedule adjustment that the life cycle then cancels out in full"]'::jsonb, 1, 'MS3''s valve maintenance is 5000.000000 a year, the lowest, so its life-cycle cost of 18953.933847 is the smallest. It delivers in 8 weeks, which is the minimum, so its schedule adjustment is 0.000000; no bid earns a credit for delivering early. MS4''s 26535.507386 is the largest life-cycle cost. MS2''s 1314.250000 is an addition for 9 weeks.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-lowest-evaluated-cost-over-the-life', 15, 'A materials report states: "The lowest evaluated cost is MS4." Which reading of that sentence does the course''s vocabulary allow?', '["Its quoted price was the lowest of the five materials bids that answered the invitation", "Once the bid below the pass mark is set aside, MS4 carries the lowest price of those left", "MS4 has the lowest evaluated cost the engine builds from price, omission, schedule and life cycle", "MS4 scores the highest combined score at a stated technical weight on the materials tender"]'::jsonb, 2, '"Lowest evaluated cost" names the evaluated cost as the engine builds it: corrected price, discount, deviations, omissions, schedule and life cycle. MS4''s quoted total is 503930.000000, and MS5 quotes less at 487200.000000. Among the four passing bids MS4 does quote the lowest total, yet that is a fact about price: the course never reads "lowest evaluated cost" as a quoted price, even where the two point at the same bid. "Most advantageous" is the term for the highest combined score, and the materials tender has no combined score.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-rated-criteria-and-price-scoring', 1, 'The well services tender is rated high procurement risk with an estimated cost of US$900000. Which para 5.50 cell does weightingBand return, and what range?', '["The cell a range, 0.500000 to 0.800000, since every high-risk contract falls in that cell of the matrix", "Low-value cell d, 0.200000 to 0.300000, since a contract of that size is low value under para 5.50", "Cell b, a Rated Criteria weighting from 0.600000 to 1.000000", "Cell c, from 0.100000 to 0.400000, a high-value cell"]'::jsonb, 2, 'High risk and an estimated cost below US$10 million give cell b: "High/Substantial Procurement Risk and Low Value, Rated Criteria weighting between 60% and 100%". Cell a is high risk and high value. Cells c and d are the Moderate/Low rows, and the risk here is high, so neither applies.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-rated-criteria-and-price-scoring', 2, 'Which pair of settings gives the narrowest weighting range of the four cells?', '["Moderate or low risk with a low-value contract, cell d at 0.200000 to 0.300000", "High risk with a low-value contract, cell b at 0.600000 to 1.000000", "High risk and high value, cell a, as both conditions pull inward", "Moderate or low risk with a high-value contract, cell c at 0.100000 to 0.400000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine returns cell d as 0.200000 to 0.300000, a width of a tenth. Cell c runs 0.100000 to 0.400000, cell a 0.500000 to 0.800000 and cell b 0.600000 to 1.000000, all of them wider.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-rated-criteria-and-price-scoring', 3, 'An evaluator passes risk as ''medium'' to weightingBand. What comes back?', '["Cell c or d, as the engine maps medium onto the Moderate/Low row", "The two cells for both rows side by side, with a note asking the evaluator to choose between them", "A refusal naming estimatedCostUsd, as risk is then read from the cost", "A refusal: \"risk must be ''high'' (High/Substantial) or ''low'' (Moderate/Low)\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine accepts two words only and maps the Regulations'' four ratings onto them: High or Substantial is high, Moderate or Low is low. Any other word is refused by the field risk with the message in the key. It returns no cell for an unknown word and never guesses a row.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-rated-criteria-and-price-scoring', 4, 'Moderate or low risk, an estimated cost of US$900000, and a stated technical weight of 0.35. What does weightingBand return?', '["Inside the band, since 0.35 sits within the range 0.100000 to 0.400000 of cell c for that risk", "Outside the band, with the reason that misapplying the matrix may lead to misprocurement (Annex X para 3.4)", "A refusal, because a technical weight outside its cell is an input error the engine will not score", "Inside the band, as the band limits only the upper end and 0.35 is below the 0.6 of cell b"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Low risk and low value give cell d, 0.200000 to 0.300000. The engine returns within band false with the reason "technical weight 0.35 is outside the range 0.2 to 0.3; misapplication of the matrix may lead to misprocurement (Annex X para 3.4)". That is a result with a reason. Cell c is the high-value row and does not apply at US$900000, and a lower end binds as firmly as an upper one.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-rated-criteria-and-price-scoring', 5, 'Under moderate or low risk, what separates a contract estimated at exactly US$10000000 from one at US$9999999?', '["Both sit in cell d, as high value begins only above US$10 million", "Both sit in cell c, since the engine rounds an estimated cost to the nearest million first", "The first is high value in cell c; the second is low value in cell d", "The first is refused on the line; the second goes to cell d"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every cell rule ends "high value means an estimated cost at or above US$10 million", and HIGH_VALUE_USD is 10000000. At exactly that figure the contract is high value (cell c, 0.100000 to 0.400000); a dollar less is low value (cell d, 0.200000 to 0.300000). Nothing is rounded and a figure on the line is placed by the "at or above" rule.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-rated-criteria-and-price-scoring', 6, 'High risk, US$12000000, and a stated technical weight of 0.5, the lower end of cell a. What does the engine say?', '["\"technical weight 0.5 is inside the range 0.5 to 0.8\"", "Outside, because a technical weight has to sit strictly above the lower end of its range", "That the weight is on the line and must be stated to more decimals before a cell can judge it", "Cell b''s range applies instead, so 0.5 is read against 0.6 to 1 for this contract"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both ends of a range are inside. The engine returns within band true with the reason quoted in the key. Cell b is the low-value row and does not apply to US$12000000, and the engine has no rule asking for more decimals.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-rated-criteria-and-price-scoring', 7, 'An evaluator hands weightingBand a technical weight of 1.2. What does the engine return?', '["The cell and its range, with within band false and a reason naming the misprocurement risk", "The cell with the weight clipped to 1, so a high-risk low-value contract still reads as inside", "A result reading the weight as 12 percent of the combined score, which is placed in cell c", "A refusal: \"technicalWeight must be a number from 0 to 1\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'A weight outside zero to one is refused by the field technicalWeight before any cell is compared with it. Within band false is what a valid weight outside its range returns, as 0.35 does in cell d. The engine does not clip or rescale a weight.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-rated-criteria-and-price-scoring', 8, 'On the four responsive well services bids (Cmin 862141, Cmax 957990), WS2''s evaluated cost is 885574.000000. What commercial score does it get under each price method?', '["75.552171 by the lowest ratio and 97.353920 by the linear method, which gives the full 100 to Cmin only", "97.353920 by the lowest ratio and 75.552171 by the linear method", "97.353920 by both methods, since WS2 sits between Cmin and Cmax and each method draws a straight line", "90.970882 by the lowest ratio and 84.430357 by the linear method, taken off the combined column"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The lowest ratio is Sc = 100 x Cmin / C, giving 97.353920. The linear method is Sc = 100 x (Cmax - C) / (Cmax - Cmin), giving 75.552171. 90.970882 and 84.430357 are WS2''s combined scores B at technical weight 0.7 with the relative technical method, where the question asks for Sc.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-rated-criteria-and-price-scoring', 9, 'At technical weight 0.7 with the relative technical method, the well services award goes to WS3 under the lowest-ratio price method. What happens under the linear method, with nothing else changed?', '["WS3 stays, since only the technical weight can move an award", "WS2 becomes most advantageous, since it gains the most from the stretched commercial scale", "WS1 becomes most advantageous at 87.162020, since the linear method rewards the middle bids", "WS5 becomes most advantageous at 87.647059, and WS3 falls to 70.000000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under the linear method the dearest responsive bid scores Sc 0.000000 and the cheapest 100.000000. WS3, the dearest, drops to a combined 70.000000 and WS5 leads at 87.647059. The price method alone moves the award. 87.162020 is WS1''s combined score at technical weight 0.85 under the linear method, a different setting.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-rated-criteria-and-price-scoring', 10, 'Kiiver and Kodym''s Table 1 (Journal of Public Procurement 15(3), Fall 2015) prices bids A, B and C at 50, 75 and 100, price only. What does the engine score B?', '["67 by the lowest ratio exactly, since the engine rounds to whole points as the source does", "66.666667 by the lowest ratio, which the source prints as 67, and 50.000000 by the linear method", "50.000000 by the lowest ratio and 66.666667 by the linear method, as B sits halfway along the line", "75.000000 by the linear method, which is the straight line drawn between A''s 100 and C''s 50"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The lowest ratio gives 100 x 50 / 75 = 66.666667; the source prints 67, and printed alike is not equal. The linear method gives the dearest bid 0.000000, so B at the midpoint scores 50.000000. The engine has no method that reproduces a 75 for B, and the course claims none.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-rated-criteria-and-price-scoring', 11, 'Chen (2008, p. 409) scores price as "50 x L / P" on prices 40, 50 and 80. Declaring A invalid after opening changes the gap between B and C. From what to what?', '["From 15.000000 to 18.750000 points, with B ahead of C both times", "From 18.750000 to 15.000000 points, as removing the lowest price compresses the field", "It stays at 15.000000 points, since A is neither B nor C and a price score reads each bid alone", "From 15.000000 to 18.750000 points, and C moves ahead of B once A has been removed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With A in the field B scores 40.000000 and C 25.000000; with A excluded B scores 50.000000 and C 31.250000. The gap widens from 15.000000 to 18.750000, and B stays ahead. A relative price score depends on which other bids are in the field. B leads C in both columns, so only the distance between them moves.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-rated-criteria-and-price-scoring', 12, 'To enter Chen''s example the course gives every bid a technicalPercent of 0 at technical weight 0.5. Why must the technical method be ''absolute''?', '["Under ''relative'' every bid takes St 100, which doubles each combined score and breaks the match with Chen", "''relative'' is kept for combined awards over 0.5, and Chen''s weight of 0.5 sits below that threshold", "Under ''relative'' Thigh is 0, and the engine refuses: \"bids all score 0 technically, so Thigh is 0 and the ''relative'' technical score is undefined\"", "Under ''relative'' the engine returns every bid with a null score and a reason that no bid passed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'St = 100 x T / Thigh needs a Thigh above 0. With every technical percent at 0 the engine refuses the call by the field bids with the message in the key. ''absolute'' uses T as scored, so B = 0.5 x 100 x Cmin / C, which is Chen''s formula. The engine has no threshold on the technical weight for either method.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-rated-criteria-and-price-scoring', 13, 'An evaluator asks rankTender for the price method ''mean-deviation''. What does the engine return?', '["Lowest-ratio ranking, taken as the default for an unknown name", "Each bid scored on its distance from the mean price, in the family Chen warns against", "A refusal naming technicalMethod, which fixes the price method", "A refusal: \"priceMethod must be ''lowest-ratio'' or ''linear''; there is no default\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine offers ''lowest-ratio'' and ''linear'' only, has no default price method, and refuses any other name by the field priceMethod. Average-price methods are described and warned against by Kiiver and Kodym and by Chen, and neither prints a formula, so the engine does not offer one.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-rated-criteria-and-price-scoring', 14, 'Stepping the technical weight under linear pricing on the well services bids (relative technical score), the stepped table prints the row at 0.85: WS1 87.162020, WS2 86.332826, WS5 85.000000, WS3 85.000000. How does the engine read that row?', '["WS1 is most advantageous; WS5 and WS3 tie, and the earlier receipt puts WS3 above WS5", "WS1 is most advantageous; WS5 and WS3 tie, and the lower evaluated cost ranks WS5 above WS3", "WS5 and WS3 share the award, since two bids with the same combined score cannot be separated", "WS3 is most advantageous, since it carries the highest technical percentage of the tied pair"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The table prints the row with "WS3 by lower evaluated cost" in the tie column: WS5 (862141.000000) is cheaper than WS3 (957990.000000), so WS5 ranks above it. The tie-break is the lower evaluated cost first, then the earlier receipt, then the bidder id, and receipt is never reached here. WS1, at 87.162020, is most advantageous at that step.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-rated-criteria-and-price-scoring', 15, 'At technical weight 0.7 the engine''s combined-score basis prints the commercial weight with a 4 in its seventeenth decimal place, where a reader expects 0.3. What does that tell a reader?', '["Seventeen significant digits, as the Standard Procurement Document states the cost weight", "A small margin added by the engine to the commercial weight so that ties on combined score are rarer", "The engine computes 1 - 0.7 in binary floating point and prints the shortest decimal of the double it holds", "The figure is the double 0.3 printed in full, as every weight is printed to its last binary digit"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course states that the engine computes 1 - 0.7 in binary floating point, prints the shortest decimal that reads back to the double it holds, and that this double is not the double 0.3. No margin is added, and the SPD formula writes the weight as (1 - X) with no digits at all.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-abnormally-low-bids', 1, 'The abnormallyLow function is handed the substantially responsive bids of a tender. What decides whether it takes the absolute or the relative approach?', '["Whether an estimate is given, since an estimate always wins over the count", "A wide spread of the bids, which switches the engine to the relative test", "The count of bids: fewer than 5 takes the absolute approach and 5 or more the relative one", "The award basis: a lowest-cost award takes the absolute test and a combined award the relative test"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine chooses by the number of substantially responsive bids, with ALB_RELATIVE_MIN_BIDS at 5, cited to Stage 1 of the ALB Guidance (2016). With 5 or more bids the relative test runs even when an estimate is given, and the engine still refuses a negative one. Neither the spread nor the award basis enters the choice.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-abnormally-low-bids', 2, 'With fewer than five substantially responsive bids, when does the engine flag a bid?', '["When 100 x (estimate - C) >= 20 x estimate, so 20% or more below the estimate", "Only if C sits more than one population standard deviation below the mean of the bids handed in", "Strictly beyond 20% below the estimate; a bid exactly at 20% is left alone", "At 100 x (estimate - C) >= 10 x estimate, the s.16 margin of the content Act applied to price"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s basis reads "fewer than 5 substantially responsive bids: flag when 100 x (estimate - C) >= 20 x estimate (20% or more below)", with ALB_ABSOLUTE_PCT at 20. The inequality includes the 20% line. The mean less one standard deviation is the relative test for 5 or more bids, and the 10 percent margin belongs to s.16 of the content Act, which protects an indigenous company.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-abnormally-low-bids', 3, 'Annex I Example 2 has four bids and a Borrower''s cost estimate of 150003863. Which bids does the engine flag?', '["Bid 1 only, the lowest, because the Guidance discusses that bid and no other in its text", "Bid 1 and Bid 3, as a bid 5.339220 percent away on either side of the estimate is examined too", "None at all, since four bids are too few for the engine to judge any price abnormally low", "Bid 1 at 42.759565 and Bid 2 at 23.005876 percent below"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Bid 1 is 42.759565 percent below and Bid 2 23.005876 percent below; both are 20 percent or more below, and the engine flags every bid the rule reaches. The Guidance discusses Bid 1, the lowest. Bid 3''s -5.339220 is a bid above the estimate. Four bids take the absolute approach and still judge every bid.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-abnormally-low-bids', 4, 'In Annex I Example 2, Bid 4''s percentage below the estimate prints as -10.254183. What does the sign mean?', '["Bid 4 is 10.254183 percent below the estimate, short of 20, so it is left unflagged", "Bid 4 sits above the estimate, so it is not flagged", "The estimate is 10.254183 percent below Bid 4''s price, so the estimate itself is flagged as low", "Bid 4 was refused, as the engine signals a price above the estimate with a negative figure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course states it: a negative percentage is a bid above the estimate. Bid 4''s evaluated cost of 165385533 is above 150003863, and its flag is false. The engine does not flag an estimate, and a flag false is a result: nothing is refused.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-abnormally-low-bids', 5, 'The abnormally low view is called on the four responsive materials bids with no estimate. What does the engine return?', '["The relative test, with a mean, a population standard deviation and a limit over the four bids", "A result with every flag false and the reason that no estimate was given for the absolute approach", "A refusal: \"estimate is required: with 4 substantially responsive bids (fewer than 5) the absolute approach compares each bid with the Borrower''s cost estimate\"", "An estimate built by the engine from the mean of the four bids, and each bid compared with it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Four bids take the absolute approach, which compares each bid with the Borrower''s cost estimate, so the estimate is required and the call is refused by the field estimate. The engine never switches to the relative test below 5 bids and never builds an estimate of its own.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-abnormally-low-bids', 6, 'Sixteen bids are handed to abnormallyLow with an estimate of -1. The relative approach will not use the estimate. What does the engine return?', '["The relative test on the sixteen bids, with the estimate ignored since the approach does not read it", "The absolute test, because an estimate given at all overrides the count of bids handed to the engine", "A result with every bid flagged, since every evaluated cost is more than 20% above a negative figure", "The call is refused, since a given estimate has to be above 0 whichever approach runs"]'::jsonb, 3, 'An estimate that is given must be valid, whatever approach runs. The engine refuses a negative estimate by the field estimate, "estimate must be a finite number above 0 when given", even with sixteen bids. The count decides the approach, and the call never reaches a test.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-abnormally-low-bids', 7, 'For five or more bids the engine flags a bid when "C < mean - SD, the population standard deviation". What does the strict inequality mean for a bid exactly at the limit?', '["It is not flagged; only a bid below mean less one standard deviation is flagged", "Flagged, because a price at the limit already counts as one standard deviation below the mean", "Refused, as the engine cannot decide a price on the line", "Flagged under the population figure and left alone under the sample figure the engine also prints"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rule is strict: C < mean - SD. A price equal to the limit fails the inequality, so it is not flagged. The engine refuses nothing on the line. It computes only the population figure for its test; the sample standard deviation is a derived comparison the course prints, and the engine never flags by it.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-abnormally-low-bids', 8, 'On Annex I Example 1, with 16 bids, what mean, standard deviation and limit does the engine return?', '["1664426, 315975 and 1348452, which the engine rounds to the unit", "Mean 1664426.375000, standard deviation 315974.537496 and limit 1348451.837504", "A standard deviation of 326337.099079 on the n - 1 rule, limit 1338089.275921", "Mean 1348451.837504, standard deviation 315974.537496 and limit 1145142, the lowest bid of the sixteen"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine returns 1664426.375000, 315974.537496 (the population figure) and 1348451.837504. The Guidance prints 1664426, 315975 and 1348452, each rounded to the unit; printed alike is not equal. 326337.099079 is the sample standard deviation, a derived comparison with a derived limit of 1338089.275921.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-abnormally-low-bids', 9, 'Example 1''s Bid 3 has an evaluated cost of 1342106. Why does the choice of standard deviation decide its flag?', '["Above both limits, so the Guidance''s own text is what flags it", "Below both limits, so the choice changes only the printed reason", "It sits below the population limit 1348451.837504 and above the sample limit 1338089.275921", "It sits exactly on the population limit, so the strict inequality alone decides it either way"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under the population standard deviation the limit is 1348451.837504 and Bid 3 at 1342106 is below it, so the engine flags Bid 1, Bid 2 and Bid 3. The sample figure, 326337.099079, would put the limit at 1338089.275921, and Bid 3 would stay unflagged with only Bid 1 and Bid 2 flagged (derived). 1342106 is on neither limit.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-abnormally-low-bids', 10, 'Why does the engine use the population standard deviation for the relative test?', '["The Guidance''s own Annex I Example 1 computes its figure that way", "The sample figure cannot be computed for fewer than twenty bids, so the population one is used", "lib/stats offers only the population figure, and the engine imports every statistic it uses", "The population figure is the larger of the two and so flags fewer honest bids by construction"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s declared choice follows the Guidance: it divides by the number of bids, as Annex I Example 1 does. lib/stats also carries sampleStandardDeviation, which gives the larger figure, 326337.099079 against 315974.537496, so the population figure is the smaller of the two here and flags more bids.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-abnormally-low-bids', 11, 'Every flag reason from abnormallyLow ends with the same clause. What does it tell the evaluator to do?', '["Reject the bid outright unless the bidder can show that it quoted its price in error during the tender", "Replace the bid''s price with the estimate and rank it again at the average of the others", "Drop the bid from the field and rerun the test, since a flagged bid distorts the mean", "Clarify the price with the bidder before any decision; the bid is never rejected automatically"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clause is "a potential abnormally low bid: clarify the price with the bidder before any decision; it is never rejected automatically". Stage 1 identifies a bid to examine, and the Guidance requires the price to be clarified with the bidder first. The engine rejects nothing on a flag, reprices nothing and drops nothing.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-abnormally-low-bids', 12, 'A committee examines a flagged bid and rejects it. How does that rejection enter the engine?', '["As a flag the engine sets on its own once a bid has stayed flagged through two runs of the test", "As a stated rejection reason on the bid at the commercial stage, which the engine excludes with that reason", "It cannot enter, since the engine holds every bid in the evaluation for as long as the tender runs", "As an evaluated cost of 0 for the bid, which the engine then reads as a withdrawal from the tender"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A bid can carry a stated rejection reason at the commercial stage, and the engine excludes it with that reason in its own words. An empty reason is refused: "bids[0].rejected must be a non-empty reason string when given". The engine never rejects on a flag by itself, and an evaluated cost of 0 is refused by name.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-abnormally-low-bids', 13, 'The World Bank Guidance on Evaluating Bids and Proposals (February 2025), Figures X to XII, ranks five companies. What does the engine do with company E?', '["E is ranked first on price, since the lowest-ratio method gives the lowest evaluated cost 100 points", "The relative test flags E, and E stays in the ranking until the committee has examined it in full detail", "A refusal comes back, because a ranking cannot run with a bid the Guidance has already set aside by name", "It excludes E with the stated reason \"abnormally low bid, rejected after examination\" and ranks D, C, B, A"]'::jsonb, 3, 'E enters the call as a rejected bid with a stated reason, so the engine excludes it with that reason and ranks the other four: D, C, B, A. The ranking agrees with the Guidance. The combined score does not run the abnormally low test, and a stated rejection is an ordinary result.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-abnormally-low-bids', 14, 'Under the relative approach, what happens to the limit when a committee removes a flagged bid from the field?', '["The mean and standard deviation are computed again over the bids left, so the limit is computed again for every bid", "Nothing moves, as the limit is fixed on the first run and read again for each remaining bid", "The limit moves only for bids that were flagged, while the unflagged bids keep their old verdict", "The engine keeps the removed bid in the statistics and drops it only from the list to clarify"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The relative test computes the mean and the population standard deviation over the bids it is handed, so a new field gives a new limit for all of them. The engine counts the bids it is handed; with fewer than five left it switches to the absolute approach and asks for an estimate.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-abnormally-low-bids', 15, 'The Guidance discusses only Bid 1 of Annex I Example 2. Why does the engine also flag Bid 2?', '["The engine flags the two lowest bids of any field, whatever percentage they sit below the estimate", "Bid 2 falls more than one standard deviation below the mean of the four bids in the example", "Bid 2 sits 23.005876 percent below the estimate, which is 20 percent or more by the same rule", "The Guidance''s own table flags Bid 2 as well, and the engine copies the Guidance''s flags as printed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine applies one rule to every bid: 100 x (estimate - C) >= 20 x estimate. Bid 2 at 23.005876 percent below meets it, so it is flagged beside Bid 1. Four bids take the absolute approach, so no mean or standard deviation is computed, and nothing is copied from the Guidance''s text.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-measuring-nigerian-content', 1, 'Which edition of the Nigerian content Schedule does the engine hold, and what does it say about later targets?', '["A yearly Schedule as amended by the Board, from its latest list", "The Schedule of the 2010 Act as enacted; later Board targets are not included", "The 2010 Schedule with every later Board target folded in as a default beside the enacted lines", "No Schedule at all: every minimum is a stated input"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s source string reads "Nigerian Oil and Gas Industry Content Development Act 2010 (Act No. 2, commenced 22 April 2010) s.11 and the Schedule as enacted in 2010 (later Board targets are not included)". No later Board target was read, so none is held; a later target enters as a user-stated target with its source. The enacted lines are held with their minimums.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-measuring-nigerian-content', 2, 'How many lines of the Schedule does NC_SCHEDULE carry, and in how many of its sections?', '["45 lines in one list with no section headings, since the engine flattens the Schedule when it loads", "24 lines, all in WELL AND DRILLING SERVICES/PETROLEUM TECHNOLOGY, the section the two tenders use", "12 lines in 3 sections, one for each item the Ekene materials tender prices in its bill", "45 lines in 3 sections, 12 of them under MATERIALS AND PROCUREMENT"]'::jsonb, 3, 'NC_SCHEDULE holds 45 lines in 3 sections: MATERIALS AND PROCUREMENT (12), WELL AND DRILLING SERVICES/PETROLEUM TECHNOLOGY (24) and EXPLORATION, SUBSURFACE, PETROLEUM ENGINEERING AND SEISMIC (9). 24 is the size of one section, and the materials tender draws four of its items from the 12-line section.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-measuring-nigerian-content', 3, 'A content item names the Schedule line ''casing-and-tubing''. What does the engine return?', '["A refusal: \"items[0].scheduleLine ''casing-and-tubing'' is not a line of NC_SCHEDULE; state targetPct, measure and source instead\"", "The nearest line the engine holds, steel-pipes at 100% by tonnage, with a note that the key was matched", "A result with a target of 0% for the item, since the Schedule sets no minimum for a line it lacks", "A refusal asking for weights, as an unknown line has to be averaged over the lines the engine holds"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A key the engine does not hold is refused by the field items[0].scheduleLine, and the message says what to do instead: state the target, its unit and its source. The engine never guesses a nearby line and never invents a minimum of 0. Weights are refused only where items in different units have none.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-measuring-nigerian-content', 4, 'How does the engine decide whether a bid meets the minimum for one item?', '["It meets when content > the minimum, so a bid exactly on the minimum falls short of the Schedule line", "It counts every unit the bid reports and converts each to spend before comparing with the minimum", "content = 100 x nigerian / total in the Schedule line''s unit; it meets when content >= the minimum", "The overall content of the bid is set against each item''s minimum, so one figure decides every item"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s basis reads "content = 100 x nigerian / total in the measured unit of the Schedule line; the item meets its minimum when content >= the minimum". The comparison includes the minimum. Each item is tested in its own unit, and the overall figure is a separate result.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-measuring-nigerian-content', 5, 'WS1''s pumping content is 95.000000 by man-hours, and the Schedule minimum for Pumping Services is 95%. What does the engine return for that item?', '["Short of it, because the comparison is strict and the reason names the item below 95%", "It meets the minimum, since an item at the minimum meets it", "Refused, as a figure on the minimum needs more decimals", "It meets only if the bid''s overall content also reaches 95%, which it does not reach here"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rule is content >= the minimum, so 95.000000 against 95% meets it, and WS1 meets 3 of 3. No reason is printed for WS1, because only items below their minimum get one. The overall content does not enter the item test; WS1''s overall is 82.647059.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-measuring-nigerian-content', 6, 'A materials bid reports its valves by tonnage. What does the engine do?', '["Refuses: \"bids[0].items.valves.measure must be ''number'', the unit the minimum is measured in; got ''tonnage''\"", "Converts the tonnage to a count of valves with the average weight of a gate valve in the bill", "Accepts it, because tonnage is one of the units NC_MEASURES lists for materials of every kind", "Tests the valves by tonnage and flags the item as below its minimum until a count is supplied"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Schedule measures Valves by number, and the engine accepts only the unit a Schedule line names for that item, so the call is refused with the message in the key. NC_MEASURES lists the units the engine knows, and a listed unit is still refused for an item the Schedule measures another way. Nothing is converted.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-measuring-nigerian-content', 7, 'Which two inputs on a content item would the engine refuse as impossible arithmetic?', '["Nigerian quantity 0, and a total larger than the quantity the tender''s bill states for the item", "A total above the bill''s quantity, and a Nigerian quantity stated with a decimal fraction of a tonne", "A Nigerian quantity equal to the total, and a total of exactly 1, since each gives a ratio at an edge", "A total of 0, and more Nigerian tonnes than the total the bid supplies"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine refuses "bids[0].items.casing.total must be a finite number above 0" and "bids[0].items.casing.nigerian must be a number from 0 to total". A Nigerian quantity of 0 and one equal to the total both sit inside 0 to total and give 0 and 100 percent: MS3''s casing is 100.000000 and meets its minimum.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-measuring-nigerian-content', 8, 'Every well services item is measured in man-hours. How does the engine compute a bid''s overall content there?', '["The plain mean of the three item contents, each item counting the same whatever its size", "The lowest of the item contents, since a bid is only as good as its weakest item", "100 x the sum of Nigerian man-hours / the sum of total man-hours, pooled over the items", "A weighted mean with the bid''s quoted amounts as weights, the rule the materials tender uses"]'::jsonb, 2, 'When every item shares one unit the engine pools: overall = 100 x sum of Nigerian quantities / sum of total quantities. Pooling weights each item by its size in man-hours. The weighted mean with stated weights is the rule for items in different units, as on the materials tender.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-measuring-nigerian-content', 9, 'WS5''s overall content is 78.750000. What does the item test add to that single figure?', '["Nothing, as an overall above every minimum shows each item meets it", "Stimulation sits at 75.000000 against a minimum of 85%, so WS5 meets 2 of 3", "All three items meet their minimums, since 78.750000 is above the coiled tubing minimum of 75%", "Coiled tubing falls short at 75.000000, at its minimum"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s reason for WS5 is "item stimulation: Nigerian content 75% by man-hours is below the minimum 85%". Coiled tubing at 75.000000 meets its 75% minimum, and pumping at 95.000000 meets 95%. The overall figure hides which item falls short, so a report gives both.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-measuring-nigerian-content', 10, 'MS2''s overall content is 61.584657. How does the engine reach it on the materials tender?', '["100 x its Nigerian tonnes over its total tonnes, with the valves left out as they are counted", "The plain mean of its four item contents, since the Act counts every Schedule line the same", "Its highest item content, cement at 82.000000", "A mean of its item contents weighted by its own quoted amount for each item"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The materials items mix tonnage and number, so the engine takes the weighted mean of the item contents with the bid''s stated weights. The fixture weights each item by the bid''s quoted amount: casing 288000, valves 103200, cement 45000, baryte 80000. That weighted mean is checked to give 61.584657. The Act gives no rule for adding man-hours to tonnes, so the weights are a stated input.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-measuring-nigerian-content', 11, 'A materials bid reports items in different units and states no weights. What comes back?', '["A refusal naming bids[0].weights, which says the overall content is a weighted mean with stated weights", "Equal weights on every item, the engine''s default when the units differ and nothing is stated", "An overall content pooled over the raw quantities, tonnes and valve counts added together", "A result with the overall content set to null and each item''s content still reported alone"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine refuses: "bids[0].weights are required: the items are measured in different units, so the overall content is the weighted mean of the item contents with stated weights (for example the priced amount of each item)". It has no default weighting, and it never adds tonnes to a count of valves.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-measuring-nigerian-content', 12, 'A bid states weights for its four materials items, and every one of them is 0. What does the engine do?', '["Falls back to equal weights, since zero weights carry no information about the items", "Returns an overall content of 0.000000 for the bid, the weighted mean of nothing", "Refuses: \"bids[0].weights must not all be 0\"", "Pools the raw quantities instead, the rule it applies when every item shares a unit"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Weights that all sum to nothing leave the weighted mean undefined, and the engine refuses them by name with the message in the key. A negative weight is refused too. It has no fallback to equal weights or to pooling for items in different units.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-measuring-nigerian-content', 13, 'The course states two bids on a land-rig Schedule line (70% by man-hours) and a catering target of 90% by man-hours. B1 sits at 70.000000 and 90.000000. What does the engine return for B1?', '["Neither is met, as a content on a minimum counts as short", "It meets both minimums, 2 of 2, with an overall content of 73.333333", "Only the rig line, as an unlisted target can be met by no bid", "Both are met, with an overall content of 73.166667 pooled across its man-hours"]'::jsonb, 1, 'B1 sits exactly on both minimums and meets both, with an overall of 73.333333. B2, at 69.900000 and 89.500000, meets 0 of 2 and has the overall 73.166667. A stated target is tested like a Schedule line once it carries its percentage, unit and source.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-measuring-nigerian-content', 14, 'What source does the engine return beside the course''s stated catering target of 90%?', '["\"stated by the user: a level stated for this example by the course; no Board target was read\"", "The Act''s s.11(2), as the Board''s power makes any stated target a Board target", "The 2010 Schedule''s EXPLORATION section, assigned to any unknown item", "No source at all, because a stated target is a figure the engine takes on trust and prints bare"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine returns every target with its source. A stated target''s source is what the user wrote, labelled "stated by the user". The rig line returns the Act''s citation with "Drilling Rigs (Land) 70% by man-hours". A stated target with no source is refused: "items[0].source must state where the target comes from (a user-stated target is never a hidden default)".'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-measuring-nigerian-content', 15, 'The Nigerian content call is handed all six well services bids, including WS4 and WS6, which fail the technical envelope. What does it return for WS4?', '["A refusal naming bids[3], as a bid that failed the technical envelope cannot be measured", "Nothing for WS4, since the call measures only bids that passed the pass mark of 70", "Its item contents with the overall content set to null, since it is out of the evaluation", "Its contents all the same: 67.058824 overall, meeting 0 of 3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The content table covers all six bids: WS4 has coiled tubing 60.000000, pumping 80.000000 and stimulation 75.000000, 0 of 3 met, overall 67.058824. The call measures the bids it is handed, and nothing in it reads the technical envelope. Which bids reach s.14 is decided at the commercial stage.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-section-14', 1, 'The engine reads s.14''s phrase "within 1 % of each other at commercial stage" in one stated way. Which?', '["As a chain of bids each within 1% of the next one, so the group can stretch well above the lowest", "Within 1% of the mean evaluated cost of the responsive bids, so the group sits around the middle", "As within 1% of the lowest evaluated cost, written 100 x (C - Cmin) <= 1 x Cmin", "As within 1% of the lowest quoted total, read before any omission, schedule or life cycle is added"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every s.14 reason prints the reading "within 1 % of each other at commercial stage" is read as within 1% of the lowest evaluated cost, and the basis writes the group as 100 x (C - Cmin) <= 1 x Cmin. A chain reading is possible and the engine does not take it. The group is measured on evaluated cost, at the commercial stage.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-section-14', 2, 'On the materials tender, MS2 sits 0.296313 percent above MS4 and MS1 3.570054 percent above. Which bids form the s.14 group, under either reading of the lead?', '["MS4 and MS2, the two bids within 1% of the lowest evaluated cost", "MS4, MS2 and MS1, the three bids within 5% of the lowest evaluated cost on the tender", "MS2 and MS3, the two bids with the highest Nigerian content among the four responsive", "It depends on the reading of the lead: points gives MS4 alone and relative gives MS4 and MS2"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The group is every bid whose evaluated cost is within 1% of the lowest, MS4 at 546244.982386. MS2 at 0.296313 percent is in; MS1 at 3.570054 and MS3 at 6.445634 are out. The engine''s table marks MS4 and MS2 in the group under both readings: the reading changes the lead test and leaves the group as it is. The 5% figure belongs to the lead test.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-section-14', 3, 'Under the points reading, stated bids LO at 2000000 (50%) and E1 at 2020000 (56%) are tested. E1 is exactly 1% above LO. What does the engine return?', '["E1 stays out of the group, since a bid must sit strictly inside 1% of the lowest to join it", "E1 joins the group, and LO stands, because a lead must exceed 5 points by a whole point", "The call is refused, as a bid exactly on the margin cannot be placed in or out of the group", "E1 joins the group, leads by 6.000000 points, and s.14 selects E1 under the points reading"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The group rule 100 x (C - Cmin) <= 1 x Cmin includes the margin, so E1 at exactly 1% is in. Read as points, its lead is 6.000000, at least 5, so s.14 applies and selects E1. At 2020001 a bid is out, and with only LO in the group the reason says "s.14 is not engaged".'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-section-14', 4, 'A stated bid E2 at 2020001 carries 90% content against LO at 2000000 with 50%, read as points. What does the engine return, and in what form?', '["E2 is selected, since a content of 90% against 50% clears 5 points by any reading of the Act", "A result with the reason that only LO is within 1% and s.14 is not engaged, so LO is selected", "A refusal naming bids[1].evaluatedCost, because E2 sits one unit outside the margin of the group", "A null lead with s.14 applied, since E2 leads on content from outside the group and cannot be ranked"]'::jsonb, 1, 'E2 is one unit beyond 1% of the lowest, so the group holds LO alone. The engine returns the result with the reason "only LO is within 1% of the lowest evaluated cost 2000000; s.14 is not engaged", and LO is selected. However high E2''s content, it never enters the comparison. That is a result with a reason, and nothing is refused.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-section-14', 5, 'How does the engine read s.14''s "its closest competitor"?', '["As the bid next above the leader in evaluated cost, since the Act acts at the commercial stage", "The bid with the lowest evaluated cost, whatever its content, since that is the bid s.14 displaces", "As the bid with the next-highest Nigerian content in the group, printed in every s.14 reason", "As the mean content of every other bid in the group, so a crowded group lowers the bar"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The reading, verbatim, is "its closest competitor" is read as the bid with the next-highest Nigerian content in that group. The competitor is found by content. The bid closest in evaluated cost is the alternative the engine does not take, and no mean enters the lead.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-section-14', 6, 'Three stated bids share the group: LO at 2000000 with 88% content, L2 at 2006000 with 70% and L3 at 2012000 with 75%. Taking the points reading, which bid is the runner-up and what is the outcome?', '["LO leads, L3 is the runner-up, the lead is 13.000000 points, and s.14 confirms LO", "LO leads, L2 is the runner-up as the bid closest to LO in price, and the lead is 18 points", "L3 is selected over LO, since s.14 prefers a bid other than the lowest when one clears 5 points", "The group holds LO alone, because L3 sits more than 1% above LO and is left out of the test"]'::jsonb, 0, 'All three sit within 1% of 2000000. The runner-up is the next-highest content, L3 at 75%, and the lead read as points is 13.000000. The engine''s reason: "LO is the lowest evaluated cost and also leads on Nigerian content, 88% against 75% (L3), a lead of 13 percentage points; s.14 confirms it". L2 is closer in price, and the engine does not read the phrase by price.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-section-14', 7, 'Does the Nigerian Oil and Gas Industry Content Development Act 2010 say whether "at least 5% higher" means five percentage points or five percent of the runner-up''s content?', '["Percentage points, as the Schedule states every minimum in points", "Five percent of the runner-up''s content, as the Act writes a % sign", "Yes: the Board''s regulations settle it as points, and the engine applies that as its default", "The Act does not say; the engine takes the reading as a stated input with no default"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every s.14 reading the engine prints ends "(the Act does not say points or relative)". The engine therefore has no default: ncLeadBasis must be stated. The course teaches both readings side by side as an open question of the Act. No later Board text was read, and the engine holds none.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-section-14', 8, 'A contentPreference call states no reading of "at least 5% higher". What does the engine return?', '["A result under the points reading, the default, with that reading printed in the s.14 reason", "A refusal by ncLeadBasis, which says s.14 does not say which reading applies, so there is no default", "Both readings side by side, since a call with no reading asks the engine to show the two outcomes", "A result with s.14 left out and the lowest evaluated cost selected, along with a note about the reading"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The message is "ncLeadBasis must be ''points'' (at least 5 percentage points more) or ''relative'' (at least 5 percent more than the runner-up''s content); s.14 does not say which, so there is no default". The engine never picks a reading for the caller. A reading misnamed as ncBasis is refused too, as a key the function does not read.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-section-14', 9, 'Under the points reading of "at least 5% higher", MS2 leads MS4 on overall content in the materials group, 61.584657 against 57.043637. What does s.14 do?', '["7.960608 points, at least 5, so s.14 selects MS2 over MS4 as the lowest evaluated cost", "MS2 is selected anyway at 4.541020 points, being inside the 1% group", "The lead is 4.541020 percentage points, less than 5, so s.14 does not apply and MS4 stands", "The lead is 4.541020 points, and MS3 is selected as the bid with the highest content on the tender"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under the points reading the engine returns a lead of 4.541020, s.14 applied false, and MS4 selected: "less than 5% higher, so the lowest evaluated cost MS4 stands". 7.960608 is the lead under the relative reading. MS3 is outside the group, so s.14 never reaches it.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-section-14', 10, 'Suppose the evaluator states the relative reading instead. MS2''s content is 61.584657 and MS4''s, the runner-up''s, 57.043637. Which bid does the engine select, and on what lead?', '["The lead is 7.960608 percent of MS4''s content, at least 5, so s.14 selects MS2 over MS4", "The lead is 4.541020 percent, short of 5, so the lowest evaluated cost MS4 stands under this reading", "The lead is 7.960608 percent, and MS4 stands anyway, since s.14 confirms the lowest in every case", "It cannot be computed, since a relative lead needs the leader''s own content as the divisor"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under the relative reading the lead is measured against the runner-up''s content: 7.960608 percent of MS4''s 57.043637, at least 5, so s.14 applies and selects MS2. The basis writes it as 100 x (top - runner-up) >= 5 x runner-up. 4.541020 is the points lead, and the divisor is the runner-up''s content.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-section-14', 11, 'Under the points reading, LO (80%) and K4 (84%) sit in the group, a lead of 4.000000 points. The same pair is then read as relative. What changes?', '["Nothing changes, because a 4-point lead falls short of 5 under the relative reading as well", "Relative gives 4.000000 percent too, so LO stands under both readings", "Both readings apply, as any positive lead in the group meets the test", "Short of 5 as points, the lead reads 5.000000 as relative and s.14 applies, selecting K4"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The edge table prints the pair twice: read as points the lead is 4.000000 and s.14 does not apply, so LO is selected; read as relative the lead is 5.000000 (4 points is 5 percent of 80) and s.14 applies, selecting K4. A lead exactly on 5 meets the test under either reading, as K5''s 5.000000 points does.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-section-14', 12, 'Under the points reading, stated bids LO (40%), S1 (65%) and S2 (65%) all sit in the group. What does the engine return?', '["A lead of 0.000000 points between S1 and S2, below 5, with the lowest evaluated cost LO kept", "A null lead with s.14 not applied, as no single bid leads, so the lowest evaluated cost LO stands", "S1 is selected, as the earlier receipt breaks the tie between the two bids at the top", "A refusal naming bids, since two bids with the same content cannot both lead the group"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s reason: "S1 and S2 share the highest Nigerian content 65%, so no single bid leads; the lowest evaluated cost LO stands". The lead is null because there is no single leader to measure it from; a lead of 0 would say a leader exists. The engine applies no tie-break to content here and refuses nothing.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-section-14', 13, 'Under the relative reading, stated bids LO (0%) and Z1 (30%) sit in the group. Why is the lead null while s.14 still applies?', '["A relative lead over a runner-up with no content would divide by zero, and any positive content is more than 5% higher", "The lead is null because Z1 is outside the group, and s.14 applies to it through s.16 instead", "No reading can compare with 0%, so the engine applies the points reading and returns its lead", "A null lead means the reading was left unstated, and the engine applies s.14 by default"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine returns the lead as null for a runner-up with no Nigerian content, since the relative lead divides by it, while any positive content is more than 5% higher by any reading. It selects Z1 with the reason "a runner-up with no Nigerian content, at least 5% higher". Z1 is in the group, and the engine never switches readings.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-section-14', 14, 'Every s.14 reason the engine returns ends with a parenthesis that opens "(readings of s.14:". What does it hold?', '["The citation of s.14 to its gazette page and the date the course read the Act", "Both outcomes, points and relative, whichever reading the call itself stated", "The three readings the engine applied: the 1% group, the closest competitor, and points or relative for the lead", "The evaluated cost and content of every bid in the group, printed at full precision"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each reason prints the three readings the call applied, the third one naming the lead basis stated for that call, so a reader holding another reading can see what was done. The points reason for MS2 and MS4 carries the points reading alone, and the relative reason the relative reading alone.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-section-14', 15, 'A contentPreference call passes the reading under the key ncBasis. What does the engine return?', '["Accepted as a short form of ncLeadBasis, so the result runs under the reading given", "The points reading, with the key ignored, since points is the engine''s default", "A refusal saying the reading must be ''points'' or ''relative'', since ncBasis held no known value", "A refusal: \"ncBasis is not an accepted key; the accepted keys at the top level are bids, ncLeadBasis\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine refuses any input key a function does not read, naming the key and the accepted keys. It never reads a misspelt key as the right one, and it has no default reading to fall back to. The refusal for a missing reading names ncLeadBasis and is a different message.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-section-16-and-the-award', 1, 'What does the engine''s s.16 rule do for a Nigerian indigenous company with capacity whose evaluated cost is within 10 percent of the lowest?', '["Selection of that bid over the lowest evaluated cost whenever its Nigerian content is also the highest", "It protects the bid from being disqualified solely because it is not the lowest; it selects nothing", "A 10 percent cut in the bid''s evaluated cost before the bids are ranked on the materials tender", "It moves the bid into the s.14 group, where the reading of the lead then decides the award"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine states s.16 as 100 x (C - Cmin) <= 10 x Cmin for a Nigerian indigenous company with capacity, and its reason ends "it is not disqualified solely because it is not the lowest (s.16)". The course states it: s.16 protects and never selects. It changes no evaluated cost, and the s.14 group is a separate test at 1%.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-section-16-and-the-award', 2, 'MS3 is stated as indigenous with capacity and sits 6.445634 percent above the lowest evaluated cost, MS4. What does the engine return for it, and does the award move?', '["Protection, and with it the award, as the only indigenous bid within the margin", "Outside the margin, because 6.445634 percent is measured against its own evaluated cost", "MS3 is placed in the s.14 group, and under the relative reading it leads with 86.240876", "MS3 is within the 10 percent margin and protected, and the award stays with the bid s.14 and the lowest evaluated cost select"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine returns MS3 with within margin true and the reason that it "is not disqualified solely because it is not the lowest (s.16)". Protection keeps a bid in the evaluation and selects nothing: the award is MS4 with no content rule or under the points reading, and MS2 under the relative reading. MS3 is outside the s.14 group at 6.445634 percent, so s.14 never reaches its 86.240876.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-section-16-and-the-award', 3, 'Stated bids: the lowest at 3000000, I10 at 3300000 and I11 one unit more, both indigenous with capacity. What does the s.16 test return?', '["I10 at 10.000000 percent is within the margin, and I11 at 10.000033 percent is outside it", "Both are outside, since the margin is strict and a bid exactly 10 percent above is left unprotected", "Both are within the margin, as the engine rounds each percentage to one decimal place first", "I10 is within and I11 is refused, because a bid just beyond the margin cannot be placed at all"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The margin includes its edge: 100 x (C - Cmin) <= 10 x Cmin. I10 sits exactly 10 percent above and is protected; I11 at 10.000033 is not. Nothing is rounded, and a bid outside the margin is an ordinary result with within margin false.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-section-16-and-the-award', 4, 'A stated bid NC sits inside the 10 percent margin and is indigenous, with capacity stated false. How does it appear in the s.16 rows?', '["Listed with within margin true, as capacity matters only for bids that sit above the 10 percent margin of s.16", "Listed with within margin false, and a reason naming the missing capacity for the committee", "It does not appear at all, since s.16 applies only to a company stated as both indigenous and with capacity", "Refused by bids[0].capacity, since an indigenous bid must have capacity to be evaluated"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine lists s.16 rows only for bids stated as indigenous and with capacity; NC is not listed. The selected bid in that stated case is LO. Capacity is a finding the caller states, and a false flag is a valid input.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-section-16-and-the-award', 5, 'An evaluator types "yes" in a bid''s indigenous flag in place of a true or false. How does the engine respond?', '["The text read as true, since any non-empty word counts as a positive finding by the committee", "It refuses by the field, since bids[0].indigenous must be true or false when given", "Read as false, so the bid is left out of the s.16 rows without any note to the evaluator", "A refusal naming capacity, as indigenous and capacity have to be given as a matched pair"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Whether a bidder is indigenous, and whether it has capacity, is stated by the caller as true or false; text is refused by the field bids[0].indigenous: "bids[0].indigenous must be true or false when given". The engine reads no truthiness into a word.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-section-16-and-the-award', 6, 'The whole tender is evaluated in one call with a combined award and a Nigerian content rule stated. What does the engine return?', '["A refusal that tells the caller to state Nigerian content as a rated criterion with its weight instead", "An award on combined score, with s.14 applied afterwards to bids within 1% on combined score", "The combined award with the content rule dropped silently and a note that s.14 applies to a lowest-cost award only", "A combined award in which each bid''s content is added to its technical percentage before scoring"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine refuses by the field nigerianContent: "nigerianContent applies s.14 at the commercial stage of a lowest-cost award; with award ''combined'' state Nigerian content as a rated criterion with its weight instead". It never applies s.14 to a combined score and never drops a stated input silently.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-section-16-and-the-award', 7, 'Under a combined award Nigerian content enters as a rated criterion. What else then binds the technical weight that carries it?', '["Nothing more: a rated criterion for Nigerian content is exempt from the Rated Criteria weighting matrix of para 5.50", "The s.14 margin, so the content criterion may weigh at most 1 percent of the combined score", "The s.16 margin, so the content criterion must carry a weight of 10 percent within the score", "Its para 5.50 cell of the World Bank Regulations (7th ed.), so the weight still sits inside the band"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A content criterion is part of the rated criteria, so the technical weight that carries it must still sit inside its para 5.50 cell, which the weighting band returns from the risk and the estimated cost. The s.14 and s.16 margins are price tests at the commercial stage and set no weight.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-section-16-and-the-award', 8, 'On the materials tender''s fixture settings, evaluateTender with a lowest-cost award is run three ways. Which awards does it return?', '["MS4 in all three runs, since s.14 cannot move a lowest-cost award away from the lowest evaluated cost at all", "MS2 in all three, as MS2 leads MS4 on Nigerian content whichever reading is stated", "MS4 with no content rule stated, MS4 under the points reading, and MS2 under the relative reading", "MS3 under both readings, as the indigenous bid with the highest content on the tender"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The end-to-end table prints: none stated gives MS4; points gives MS4; relative gives MS2 with the s.14 reason as the award reason. The lowest evaluated cost is MS4 in every run. MS3 is protected by s.16 and selected by nothing.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-section-16-and-the-award', 9, 'Under the points reading, s.14 is tested on the materials tender and does not apply. What award reason does the engine print?', '["The s.14 reason with the lead of 4.541020, since a tested section always supplies the award reason", "\"MS4 has the lowest evaluated cost\", the same reason as a run with no content rule stated", "A reason naming MS2 as leader, since MS2 heads the group on content under either reading", "A reason saying s.14 is not engaged, since a lead below 5 points leaves the group unformed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The table prints the award reason "MS4 has the lowest evaluated cost" for both the run with no content rule and the points run. Under the points reading the group is formed and MS2 leads, but the lead of 4.541020 is less than 5, so the award reason is the lowest evaluated cost''s. "Not engaged" is the wording for a group of one bid.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-section-16-and-the-award', 10, 'Stating the relative reading moves the materials award. How is the move explained in the engine''s output?', '["The s.14 reason itself, readings included, which ends with s.14 selecting MS2 over the lowest evaluated cost MS4", "\"MS2 has the lowest evaluated cost\", since the award moves to MS2 under the relative reading", "\"MS4 has the lowest evaluated cost\", followed by a note that s.14 changed the bid selected", "A reason naming MS3, the indigenous bid protected by s.16 inside the 10 percent margin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under the relative reading the award reason is the s.14 reason, which begins "2 bids within 1% of the lowest evaluated cost" and ends its finding with "at least 5% higher, so s.14 selects MS2 over the lowest evaluated cost MS4", before its readings. MS2 does not have the lowest evaluated cost; MS4 does, at 546244.982386.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-section-16-and-the-award', 11, 'With a Nigerian content rule stated, one bid that reaches the commercial stage carries no ncPct. What does evaluateTender return?', '["That bid''s content taken as 0, which puts it at the bottom of any s.14 comparison", "The bid left out of the s.14 group, with the reason that its content is unknown", "A result with s.14 skipped for the whole tender and the lowest evaluated cost as the award", "A refusal: \"bids[4].ncPct is required for every bid that reaches the commercial stage when nigerianContent is given\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every bid that reaches the commercial stage must carry its overall content once the content rule is stated, and the engine refuses by the field bids[4].ncPct with the message in the key. A missing figure is never read as 0, and nothing is skipped silently.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-section-16-and-the-award', 12, 'A lowest-cost evaluateTender call gives nigerianContent as a plain text string. What comes back?', '["The points reading, the default when the object cannot be read as it is", "Lowest evaluated cost as the award, with the content rule ignored", "A refusal by nigerianContent: it must be { ncLeadBasis } when given", "A refusal naming award, since content with text in it forces a combined award"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine refuses: "nigerianContent must be { ncLeadBasis } when given". There is no default reading to fall back to, and a stated input is never dropped. The award basis is a separate required input with its own refusal.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-section-16-and-the-award', 13, 'Which of the materials bids has the highest overall Nigerian content, and why does s.14 never compare it?', '["MS2 at 61.584657, which s.14 compares with MS4 as leader of the group of two", "MS3 at 86.240876, since at 6.445634 percent above MS4 it sits outside the 1% group", "MS3 at 86.240876, since an indigenous bid is judged under s.16 and excluded from s.14", "MS5 at 49.874791, since a bid that fails the pass mark is left out of the content test"]'::jsonb, 1, 'MS3''s overall content, 86.240876, is the highest of the five. The s.14 group holds only bids within 1% of the lowest evaluated cost, MS4 and MS2, and MS3 sits 6.445634 percent above. Nothing in s.14 excludes an indigenous bid; MS3 is simply outside the margin. MS5''s 49.874791 is the lowest content.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-section-16-and-the-award', 14, 'Suppose ncPct is typed as 101 on one bid in the s.14 and s.16 call. What comes back?', '["Content clipped to 100, with a note in the reasons that the figure was capped", "The bid may lead any group it joins, since a content above 100 clears every lead", "A refusal naming the Schedule line, since a content above 100 must come from a stated target", "The call is refused by bids[0].ncPct, whose content must sit from 0 to 100"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Content is a percentage, and the engine refuses a figure outside 0 to 100 by the field bids[0].ncPct: "bids[0].ncPct must be a number from 0 to 100". It clips nothing. A stated target is an input to the content measurement, where no content above its total can arise.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-section-16-and-the-award', 15, 'The engine keeps s.14 and s.16 apart in its output. What does each return?', '["s.14 a group, leader, runner-up, lead and selected bid; s.16 rows for indigenous bids with capacity, each with its percentage and margin flag", "One combined ranking, in which the s.16 protection adds points to a bid''s lead under s.14", "s.14 rows for every bid in the tender, and s.16 a single selected bid within 10 percent", "A shared lead figure used by both sections, read as points in s.14 and relative in s.16"]'::jsonb, 0, 'contentPreference returns the s.14 group, leader, runner-up, lead and outcome with its readings, the s.16 rows and the bid selected. s.16 lists each indigenous bid with capacity with its percentage above the lowest and whether it is within the margin, and it selects nothing. s.16 carries no lead and no reading.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'Opening MS4''s life-cycle cost year by year: which discounted figure belongs to the second year of maintenance?', '["6363.636364, since a payment made during year 2 is discounted from the end of year 1", "4781.094188, as the factor for year 2 is 1 / (1 + 0.1) raised to the fourth power", "5785.123967, the 7000.000000 times the year 2 factor 0.826446", "5259.203606, taken from the middle of the five-year life cycle as its representative year"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Year n carries the factor 1 / (1 + 0.1) raised to n, applied at the end of that year, so the second year''s 7000.000000 counts for 5785.123967. The other figures are other rows of the same table: 6363.636364 belongs to the first year, 5259.203606 to the third and 4781.094188 to the fourth.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'An evaluator states a life-cycle discount rate of -1. What does evaluatedCosts return?', '["A refusal by lifeCycle.discountRate: a fraction above -1 and at most 1, where 0.1 is 10 percent a year", "A life-cycle cost with every year''s cost doubled, since a rate of -1 inflates each future payment", "The life cycle dropped, and the evaluated cost returned without its last term for every bid", "A refusal by lifeCycle.years, as a negative rate is read as a request for no years at all"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s message is "lifeCycle.discountRate must be a fraction above -1 and at most 1 (0.1 is 10 percent a year)". A rate of -1 is outside that range and the call is refused by that field. The engine drops nothing silently, and the years have their own refusal.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'A call to evaluatedCosts spells the life-cycle key as lifecycle, with a small c. What happens?', '["Matched to lifeCycle, as input keys are compared without case", "Left out without a word, so each evaluated cost omits its last term", "The evaluated costs come back with a warning that one key was unknown and has been ignored", "The call is refused as a key the function does not read, and the accepted keys are listed"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine refuses: "lifecycle is not an accepted key; the accepted keys at the top level are bids, omissionRule, bestEstimates, schedule, lifeCycle, tolerance". A misspelt optional key is refused and never silently drops a term, which is the danger with a life cycle: the evaluated cost would look complete.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'Without the life cycle, MS3''s evaluated cost is 562500.000000, its corrected price. Why does nothing else enter?', '["MS3 is an indigenous company with capacity, and s.16 lifts every adjustment from its evaluated cost", "MS3 omits no item and delivers in 8 weeks, the minimum, so no omission and no schedule term is added", "MS3''s discount cancels its schedule adjustment of 2701.500000 to the last unit, so the sum is its price", "Without a life cycle the engine prices each bid at its quoted total, and every other term waits for it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'MS3 prices every line, so its omission term is 0.000000, and 8 weeks is not beyond minWeeks 8, so its schedule adjustment is 0.000000. Other bids carry terms without the life cycle: MS4''s 519709.475000 includes its omission 12000.000000 and schedule 3779.475000. 2701.500000 is MS1''s schedule term, and s.16 adjusts no evaluated cost.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'MS2''s evaluated cost is 547863.577232 and it carries no omission. Which terms make it up?', '["Corrected price 525700.000000 and the inspection average 12000.000000, with no schedule term at all", "Its quoted total less a discount, plus a life-cycle cost of 26535.507386 on its valve maintenance", "Corrected price 503930.000000 with its omission, schedule and life cycle added in turn", "Corrected price 525700.000000, schedule adjustment 1314.250000 and life-cycle cost 20849.327232"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The materials table prints MS2''s terms: corrected price 525700.000000, omissions 0.000000, schedule adjustment 1314.250000 and life-cycle cost 20849.327232, summing to 547863.577232. 12000.000000 is MS4''s omission, 503930.000000 is MS4''s corrected price and 26535.507386 is MS4''s life-cycle cost.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'MS1''s evaluated cost is 543001.500000 without the life cycle. What is it with the life cycle at 5 years and 0.1?', '["565746.220616, after its life-cycle cost of 22744.720616 is added", "543001.500000 still, since MS1 prices every line and omits nothing for the life cycle to add to", "565746.220616 less 22744.720616, since the life cycle is a credit taken off a bid''s price", "581453.933847, since the life cycle moves MS1 to the last rank of the four on the tender"]'::jsonb, 0, 'MS1''s valve maintenance of 6000.000000 a year discounts to a life-cycle cost of 22744.720616, and the evaluated cost rises from 543001.500000 to 565746.220616. The life cycle is a cost, and it applies to every bid whether or not it omits anything. 581453.933847 is MS3''s evaluated cost; MS1 stays third.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'The World Bank Guidance on Evaluating Bids and Proposals (February 2025), Figures X to XII, prints company D''s combined score as 98.34. The engine computes 98.333333. How should a report treat the two?', '["Equal, since two figures that agree to the second decimal place must describe the same bid''s combined score", "As different figures: the Guidance rounded up at two decimals, and the engine''s figure is exact", "An engine defect, since a combined score must reproduce the Guidance''s published figure to every printed digit", "As the Guidance''s error, since 98.34 cannot come from any rounding of the figure 98.333333"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s 98.333333 is exact; the Guidance prints to two decimals and rounded D''s figure up. The ranking agrees, D, C, B, A, and every printed combined figure is within 0.01 of the engine''s. Printed alike is not equal, and a printed source figure is quoted as the source''s.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'Annex 3 of the World Bank Guidance on Evaluating Bids and Proposals (February 2025) weights the technical score 0.4. The engine gives company A a combined 94.375000. What does the Guidance print, and how was it reached?', '["94.375000 in full, since the Guidance prints the engine''s figure to six decimals", "90.625000, company A''s financial score Sc, printed in the place of its combined figure", "94.37, two decimals of the engine''s figure with the third one dropped", "91.66, which is company A''s figure once the financial score takes 0.6"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Guidance prints 94.37, which is 94.375000 with the third decimal dropped. 91.66 is what the Guidance prints for company B, whose engine figure is 91.666667. A wins on both, with St 100.000000 and Sc 90.625000.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'A contract rated Moderate risk is estimated at US$12000000. What weighting does the Rated Criteria matrix allow it?', '["Cell c, a range of 0.100000 to 0.400000 for the Rated Criteria", "The narrowest band, cell d at 0.200000 to 0.300000, since low risk always takes it", "Top-row cell a, 0.500000 to 0.800000, since high value lifts any risk into it", "Cell b, 0.600000 to 1.000000, the band the well services tender sits inside"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine maps Moderate onto its low row, and US$12000000 is at or above the US$10 million line, so the contract is high value: the rule reads "Moderate/Low Procurement Risk and High Value, Rated Criteria weighting between 10% and 40%". Low risk does not always give the narrowest band; cells a and b belong to the High/Substantial row.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'Under linear pricing with the relative technical score, the course steps the technical weight on the well services bids from 0.7 to 1. What does the stepped table show about the award as the technical weight rises?', '["Straight from WS5 to WS3, since WS3 has the highest technical percentage", "WS5 keeps it at every step, since the linear method gives WS5 a commercial 100", "It moves from WS3 to WS1 and then to WS5 as the technical share of the score grows", "It moves from WS5 to WS1 and then to WS3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The table prints WS5 most advantageous at 0.7, 0.75 and 0.8, WS1 at 0.85 and 0.9, and WS3 at 0.95 and 1. The award passes through WS1 before WS3. At 0.85 the table also prints a tie between WS5 and WS3, both at 85.000000, ordered by the lower evaluated cost.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, 'At technical weight 0.7 and relative technical scoring, WS1''s commercial score is 92.883107 by the lowest ratio. What is it by the linear method?', '["92.883107 as well, since WS1 sits between the two extremes where the methods agree", "31.080136, as the linear line runs from 100 at Cmin to 0 at Cmax", "77.265217, which is the commercial score WS1 is given under the linear method", "0.000000, since the linear method scores every bid other than the lowest at 0"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sc linear = 100 x (Cmax - C) / (Cmax - Cmin); WS1 at 928200.000000 scores 31.080136. 77.265217 is WS1''s combined score B under linear pricing, a different figure. Only the dearest responsive bid, WS3, scores 0.000000 under the linear method.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'The well services tender is high risk at US$900000, so its cell is b. The stepped table under linear pricing runs technical weights from 0.7 to 1. What does the band say about those steps?', '["Only 0.7 sits inside cell b, so every higher step is refused by the engine as a misapplication of the matrix", "The steps from 0.85 upward fall outside cell b, which caps the Rated Criteria weight at 0.8", "Every step sits inside cell b, 0.6 to 1, so the band alone cannot choose between WS5, WS1 and WS3", "Every step sits inside cell a, since the estimated cost puts the contract on the high-value side"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Cell b runs from 0.600000 to 1.000000, and both ends are inside. Each step from 0.7 to 1 sits inside it, and across those steps the table''s award moves from WS5 to WS1 to WS3. The band limits the weight; the choice inside it is stated. 0.8 is the top of cell a, which needs a high-value contract.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'At technical weight 0.7 with linear pricing and the relative technical score, WS3''s combined score is exactly 70.000000. Why?', '["A cap at the technical weight times 100, which the engine applies to every bid whenever the linear method is chosen", "WS3''s commercial score is 70 under the linear method, and its technical score adds nothing to the combined figure", "The linear method replaces the combined score with the technical weight for every bid", "WS3 has the highest technical percentage, St 100, and the dearest cost, Sc 0, so B is 0.7 x 100"]'::jsonb, 3, 'WS3 carries the highest technical percentage, 85.000000, so St = 100 x T / Thigh is 100.000000, and it is the dearest responsive bid, so its linear Sc is 0.000000. B is 0.7 x 100 plus the commercial weight times 0, which is 70.000000. No cap exists, and the other bids score other figures: WS5 87.647059 at the same settings.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'On Annex I Example 1, Bid 4''s evaluated cost is 1378232. Is it flagged under the relative test?', '["No, since it sits above the limit of 1348451.837504, the mean less one population standard deviation", "Yes, since it sits below the mean of 1664426.375000, which is where the test draws its line", "Yes, since it is among the four lowest of the sixteen, and the test flags the lowest quarter", "No, because it sits more than 20 percent below the mean and the absolute test applies instead"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The relative test flags C < mean - SD, and the limit is 1348451.837504. Bid 4 at 1378232 is above it, so its flag is false; the engine flags Bid 1, Bid 2 and Bid 3 only. The mean itself is not the line, no quartile rule exists, and sixteen bids take the relative approach.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'An evaluator holds five substantially responsive bids and also types in a valid cost estimate. Does the estimate change which abnormally low test runs?', '["The absolute approach, since a stated estimate always takes precedence over the count of bids", "Both approaches, with a bid flagged only if each of the two tests flags it at the same time", "The relative approach, since 5 or more bids take it and the estimate is left unused", "Absolute, since the relative test needs sixteen bids like Annex I"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ALB_RELATIVE_MIN_BIDS is 5, so five bids take the relative test, comparing each bid with the mean less one population standard deviation of the field. The estimate stays unused, though it is still checked: a negative one would be refused. No test runs both ways, and Annex I Example 1 simply happens to hold sixteen bids.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'The materials tender has five bids, yet the abnormally low test on its evaluation must take the absolute approach and needs an estimate. Why?', '["Materials tenders always take the absolute approach, since the relative test is written for works", "The life cycle makes the evaluated costs present values, which the relative test cannot compare", "MS3 is indigenous with capacity, and s.16 removes it from the count the relative test reads", "MS5 failed the pass mark, so only four bids are substantially responsive, and fewer than 5 take the absolute approach"]'::jsonb, 3, 'MS5 scores 50.000000 against a pass mark of 60, so its price envelope stays shut and MS1 to MS4 remain. Four is below ALB_RELATIVE_MIN_BIDS, so the absolute test runs and a cost estimate must be typed in before any flag can be read. Nothing about materials, the life cycle or s.16 enters the count.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'The engine''s reason for Example 1''s Bid 1 prints the standard deviation as the shortest round-trip decimal of the double the engine holds, with ten decimal places. Which standard deviation does a report quote?', '["The figure inside the reason, with every digit it prints", "The numeric field at six decimals, 315974.537496, with the reason quoted only whole", "315975, the Guidance''s printed figure, which is the published value a report should cite", "326337.099079, the sample standard deviation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A figure inside a reason is the shortest round-trip decimal of the double; a report quotes the numeric field, 315974.537496, and shows a reason only verbatim. 315975 is the Guidance''s rounding to the unit, and 326337.099079 is the sample figure, which the engine does not use.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'In Annex I Example 2, a committee rejects Bid 1 after examination and the absolute test is run again on Bids 2 to 4 with the same estimate. What happens to Bid 2''s flag?', '["It stays true, since the absolute test compares Bid 2 with the estimate alone", "Cleared, as Bid 2 is now the lowest and judged against the rest", "A refusal replaces it, as three bids are too few for any approach the engine offers", "It clears, as a rejection above it resets every flag the committee has not yet examined"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under the absolute approach each bid is tested against the estimate: Bid 2 is 23.005876 percent below 150003863, which is 20 percent or more, whoever else is in the field. Fewer than 5 bids still take the absolute approach and need the estimate, which is given.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'The constants ALB_ABSOLUTE_PCT (20) and ALB_RELATIVE_MIN_BIDS (5) sit in the engine''s DEFAULTS. Where does each come from?', '["Both are engine conventions with no published source, stated so that a reader can change them", "The 20 from the Public Procurement Act 2007 s.31 and the 5 from the World Bank Regulations para 5.50", "The 20 from s.16 of the content Act and the 5 from the lead of s.14, both applied to the price", "Both from Stage 1 of the World Bank ALB Guidance, Second Edition, July 2016"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The DEFAULTS table cites both to "WB ALB Guidance (2016) Stage 1": 20 percent or more below the cost estimate for the absolute approach, and 5 substantially responsive bids as the fewest for the relative approach. The s.14 lead is NC_LEAD_PCT, the s.16 margin is INDIGENOUS_MARGIN_PCT at 10, and the Act 2007 s.31 covers arithmetic errors and deviations.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'WS2''s content is 70.000000 for coiled tubing, 90.000000 for pumping and 87.500000 for stimulation, all by man-hours. How many items meet their Schedule minimums?', '["3 of 3, since its overall content of 77.647059 is above the lowest minimum of the three", "2 of 3: coiled tubing and stimulation meet theirs, and pumping alone falls short of 95%", "1 of 3: stimulation meets 85%, while coiled tubing misses 75% and pumping misses 95%", "0 of 3, since each item is judged against the highest minimum, 95% for pumping"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each item is tested against its own line: 70.000000 against 75% falls short, 90.000000 against 95% falls short, and 87.500000 against 85% meets. The engine prints both shortfalls as reasons. The overall figure, 77.647059, does not enter the item test.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'Which Schedule line, minimum and measured unit does the materials tender tie its casing item to?', '["valves, at a minimum of 60% counted by number of joints", "steel-pipes, Steel Pipes at a minimum of 100% by tonnage", "casing-and-tubing, a line the engine holds at 100% by tonnage", "cement-portland, at 80% by tonnage, the line for well construction"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fixture ties casing to the Schedule line steel-pipes, which the engine holds at 100% by tonnage under MATERIALS AND PROCUREMENT. casing-and-tubing is not a line of NC_SCHEDULE and is refused. Valves (60% by number) and cement-portland (80% by tonnage) are the lines of other items.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'MS3''s casing content is 100.000000 against the Steel Pipes minimum of 100%. How does the engine judge the item, and MS3 overall?', '["Short of it, since no content can exceed a minimum of 100%, which leaves MS3 meeting just 3 of its 4 items", "Refused, since a content of 100% leaves no share for the total to divide", "The item meets its minimum, and MS3''s overall content is 100.000000 as its largest item", "The item meets its minimum, and MS3 meets all 4 of 4, with an overall content of 86.240876"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The rule is content >= the minimum, so 100.000000 meets 100%. MS3 meets every minimum, 4 of 4, and its weighted overall content is 86.240876. A Nigerian quantity equal to the total is inside 0 to total and is accepted.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'Which units does the engine accept for a content item, as NC_MEASURES lists them?', '["man-hours, tonnage, spend, length, number, volume and litres", "man-hours, tonnage and number only, the three units the two Ekene tenders use", "any unit the bidder states, as long as it is the same unit for every item", "spend alone, since the Act measures all content in money for comparison"]'::jsonb, 0, 'NC_MEASURES lists seven units: man-hours, tonnage, spend, length, number, volume, litres. A stated target in any other unit is refused: "items[0].measure must be one of man-hours, tonnage, spend, length, number, volume, litres". Even a listed unit is refused for an item whose Schedule line names another.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'A content item is written with a measure and a source but no targetPct and no scheduleLine. What does the engine return?', '["A target of 0% for the item, since a missing percentage is read as no minimum at all", "The nearest Schedule line''s minimum, matched from the measure and the source text", "A refusal: \"items[0].targetPct must be a number from 0 to 100 when no scheduleLine is given\"", "A result with the item marked as met by every bid, as nothing can fall below no target"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A stated target must carry a percentage, a unit and a source. Without a Schedule line the percentage is required, and the engine refuses by the field items[0].targetPct. It never sets a hidden target and never guesses a Schedule line.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'One materials bid reports casing, valves and cement but leaves baryte out of its items. What does the engine return?', '["The bid''s overall content computed over the three items it does report", "A refusal by that bid''s items, since every bid reports every item", "A baryte content of 0.000000 for that bid, with the item marked below its minimum", "A result with the missing item skipped and a reason noting it for the committee"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course''s own case leaves casing out: "bids[0].items.casing is missing: every bid reports every item". The rule is the same for any item. A missing item is never read as 0 and never skipped, because either would change the overall content.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'A materials bid reports a content item called pipe, which the tender does not list. What does the engine do?', '["Ignored as extra, with the overall content computed from the four items the tender itself lists for the bids", "Pipe joins the overall content with a weight of 0, so the figure does not move", "It maps pipe to the steel-pipes line and tests it against the Steel Pipes minimum", "It refuses: pipe is not an item id, and the accepted keys are casing, valves, cement, baryte"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine checks every item key against the item ids the call carries: "bids[0].items.pipe is not an item id; the accepted keys of bids[0].items are the item ids casing, valves, cement, baryte". An unknown key is refused and never dropped or remapped.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'On the well services tender, which bid has the highest overall content pooled by man-hours, and what is it?', '["WS3, at 86.529412, meeting 3 of 3", "WS6, at 84.705882, the highest since WS6 scores the most technically", "WS1, at 82.647059, since its pumping sits exactly on its minimum", "WS5, at 78.750000, the bid with the lowest evaluated cost on the tender"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The pooled figures are WS3 86.529412, WS6 84.705882, WS1 82.647059, WS5 78.750000, WS2 77.647059 and WS4 67.058824. WS3 is highest and meets all three minimums. WS6 fails a mandatory requirement and is not scored technically, and content does not depend on the evaluated cost.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'The engine''s constants NC_PRICE_MARGIN_PCT and NC_LEAD_PCT are both cited to s.14. What does each set?', '["1 sets the lead in points and 5 sets the price group, as a percent of the mean evaluated cost", "Both set price margins, 1 for s.14 and 5 for s.16, measured from the lowest evaluated cost", "1 sets the price group, within 1% of the lowest; 5 sets the lead, in points or relative as the caller states", "5 sets the lead in percentage points, the Act''s settled meaning, and 1 sets the price group"]'::jsonb, 2, 'NC_PRICE_MARGIN_PCT is 1, the s.14 price group within that percent of the lowest evaluated cost. NC_LEAD_PCT is 5, the s.14 content lead "at least this much higher (points or relative, stated by the caller)". The Act does not say which reading applies. The s.16 margin is INDIGENOUS_MARGIN_PCT at 10.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'K5 at 2010000 carries 75% content against LO''s 70% at 2000000. With the lead read in percentage points, what does the engine return?', '["A lead of 5.000000 points, short of the test, since the lead has to sit strictly above 5 points", "A lead of exactly 5.000000 points, which meets the test, so s.14 applies and selects K5", "A relative lead measured against LO''s content, since the engine reports every lead as relative", "No lead at all, since K5 sits 0.5% above LO and so falls outside the 1% group of the section"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The points basis is top - runner-up >= 5 percentage points, and 75 against 70 is exactly 5.000000, so s.14 applies and K5 is selected. The call states the points reading, so the lead is in points. K5 at 2010000 is within 1% of 2000000 and in the group.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'Of s.14''s three phrases, which does the engine read as a stated reading in every result, and which must the caller state?', '["\"within 1 %\" and \"closest competitor\" are read as stated; \"at least 5% higher\" is the required input ncLeadBasis", "All three are required inputs, since the Act leaves every phrase of s.14 open", "\"closest competitor\" is required, and the other two are read as printed", "None of the three is an input, as the engine applies the Board''s reading of each"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine prints its readings of "within 1 % of each other at commercial stage" (within 1% of the lowest evaluated cost) and "its closest competitor" (the next-highest Nigerian content in the group) in every reason. For "at least 5% higher" it has no default, because the Act does not say points or relative, and ncLeadBasis must be stated.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'Which content figure does the s.14 comparison of MS2 against MS4 use for MS2?', '["The valves content alone, 58.333333, since valves are the one item that s.14 of the Act names for the test", "Cement, at 82.000000, the highest of its four item contents", "Its casing content, 60.000000, since steel pipes carry the largest spend", "Its overall content, 61.584657, the mean of its items weighted by its quoted amounts"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The s.14 call carries each bid''s overall content, the figure the content call returns: MS2 61.584657 and MS4 57.043637. The overall is the weighted mean of the item contents with the bid''s quoted amounts as weights, since the items mix tonnage and number. The s.14 call reads one content figure per bid, its ncPct.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'The s.14 group is judged on evaluated costs. Which figures place MS2 0.296313 percent above MS4 on the materials tender?', '["Quoted totals, MS4 503930.000000 and MS2 525700.000000, read before any term is added", "Costs without the life cycle, MS4 519709.475000 and MS2 527014.250000", "The evaluated costs with the life cycle: MS4 546244.982386 and MS2 547863.577232", "The life-cycle costs alone, MS4 26535.507386 and MS2 20849.327232"]'::jsonb, 2, 's.14 acts at the commercial stage on the evaluated cost, which on the materials tender carries the omission, the schedule and the life cycle. MS2 at 547863.577232 sits 0.296313 percent above MS4 at 546244.982386. The figures without the life cycle and the quoted totals are other quantities, and the life-cycle cost is one term.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'A contentPreference call carries a bid whose receivedAt is "2027-03-02T13:25:00" with no zone. What comes back?', '["Read as UTC, since a receipt given with no zone is taken to be Greenwich time", "A refusal: \"bids[0].receivedAt must be a UTC time ''YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ''\"", "The bid moved to the end of any tie-break, as its receipt time cannot be compared", "A result with the receipt time ignored, since s.14 does not order bids by receipt"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine refuses a receipt time without its zone by the field bids[0].receivedAt, in contentPreference as in evaluatedCosts. It assumes no zone and never quietly drops a field it reads.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'MS3 sits 6.445634 percent above the lowest evaluated cost. How do s.14 and s.16 each treat it?', '["Outside the 1% s.14 group, so s.14 never compares it, and inside the 10 percent s.16 margin, so it is protected", "Inside both, since the Act counts an indigenous company''s price at a discount of 10 percent", "Outside both, since 6.445634 percent is above the 5% lead that s.14 and s.16 share", "Inside the s.14 group, where it leads with 86.240876 and is selected under both readings"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The s.14 group is within 1% of the lowest, and MS3 at 6.445634 percent is out, so its 86.240876 content never enters the comparison. s.16 protects an indigenous company with capacity within 10 percent, and MS3 is within it. The 5% belongs to the s.14 lead, and no price is discounted.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'The whole materials tender is sent to evaluateTender with no award basis stated. What does the engine return?', '["The award on the lowest evaluated cost, the default for a tender without rated criteria", "A combined award at technical weight 0.7, the weight that the well services tender states in its fixture", "The call is refused by award, which has no default and must be lowest-cost or combined", "The technical envelope only, with the commercial stage left for a later call"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The award basis is a required input with no default: "award must be ''lowest-cost'' or ''combined''; there is no default". The engine decides nothing a rule does not state: the pass mark, the weights, the technical weight, the methods, the s.14 reading and the award basis all have to be stated.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'Suppose the well services tender wanted Nigerian content to count under its combined award at technical weight 0.7. How can it, and what binds the weight?', '["Through s.14 at the commercial stage, applied to bids within 1% on combined score", "Through s.16, with each indigenous bid''s combined score lifted by 10 percent", "As a stated nigerianContent reading, after which the para 5.50 band no longer applies", "As a rated criterion with its own weight in the technical envelope, with the technical weight still inside cell b"]'::jsonb, 3, 'With a combined award the engine refuses the content rule and asks for Nigerian content as a rated criterion with its weight. A content criterion is a rated criterion, so the technical weight carrying it must sit inside the para 5.50 cell; for high risk at US$900000 that is cell b, 0.600000 to 1.000000, which holds 0.7.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'On the fixture settings and under the relative reading the materials award goes to MS2. In that run, which bid does the engine name as the lowest evaluated cost?', '["MS2, since the bid awarded under a lowest-cost basis is by definition the lowest evaluated cost", "MS4, at 546244.982386; the award moves by s.14 and the lowest evaluated cost stays with MS4", "MS2, once its content lead is subtracted from its evaluated cost as a price preference", "No bid, since s.14 replaces the lowest evaluated cost with the content ranking"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The end-to-end table prints MS4 as the lowest evaluated cost in every run, and MS2 as the award under the relative reading. "Lowest evaluated cost" names the evaluated cost and does not name the award. The engine applies no price preference to content.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'Four percentages sit in the Professional rules: 1, 5, 10 and 20. Which pairing matches the engine''s constants?', '["1 the s.14 group, 5 the s.14 lead, 10 the s.16 margin, 20 the absolute abnormally low test", "The s.14 group at 1, the relative ALB count at 5, the s.14 lead at 10 and the s.16 margin at 20 percent", "s.16''s margin 1, the s.14 lead 5, the absolute ALB test 10, the s.14 group 20", "1 the ALB tolerance, 5 the s.16 margin, 10 the s.14 lead, 20 the s.14 group"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The constants are NC_PRICE_MARGIN_PCT 1 and NC_LEAD_PCT 5 (s.14), INDIGENOUS_MARGIN_PCT 10 (s.16) and ALB_ABSOLUTE_PCT 20 (the ALB Guidance, Stage 1). The 5 in ALB_RELATIVE_MIN_BIDS is a count of bids, a different quantity.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'On the fixture settings with no content rule stated, the materials award reason reads "MS4 has the lowest evaluated cost". Which option gives a true reason for passing over each of the two bids it names?', '["MS2, whose Nigerian content lead is applied in every run, and MS1, which delivers late", "MS3, whose s.16 protection outranks price, and MS5, which is protected the same way", "MS5, whose lower quoted total was never opened, and MS3, whose technical 90.000000 earns only a pass", "MS2 and MS1, since the award goes to the bid with the fewest terms added"]'::jsonb, 2, 'MS5 quotes 487200.000000 but fails the pass mark of 60, so its commercial envelope is not opened. MS3 scores 90.000000, the highest, and under a lowest-cost award a technical score earns a pass and nothing more. s.14 is not tested with no content rule stated, and s.16 protects and selects nothing.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'The whole materials tender in one call, with a lowest-cost award: at which stage is MS5 excluded, and with what reason?', '["At the commercial stage, as its price is the lowest and is examined as abnormally low first", "At the s.14 stage, as its content of 49.874791 is the lowest and falls out of the group", "It is not excluded; it is ranked last after its technical shortfall is priced as a deviation", "At the technical stage: \"technical score 50 is below the pass mark 60; the commercial envelope is not opened\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'evaluateTender runs the technical envelope on every bid, then the commercial envelope on the bids that passed. MS5 fails the pass mark and is excluded at the technical stage with the reason in the key. Its content never matters to s.14, and the ALB test is a separate call.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'Under a lowest-cost award on the well services tender, which bid does evaluateTender award, and how does that compare with the combined award at 0.7, lowest-ratio and relative?', '["WS3 both ways, as the highest technical percentage", "WS5 on the lowest evaluated cost, 862141.000000, while the combined award goes to WS3", "WS4, whose quoted 763200.000000 is the lowest of the six", "WS5 both ways, since the lowest evaluated cost always scores the highest combined score"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The lowest-cost award goes to WS5, 862141.000000; the combined award at technical weight 0.7, lowest-ratio and relative goes to WS3 at 96.998434. WS4 fails the pass mark, so its quoted total is never opened. The Professional tier follows the lowest-cost road on the materials tender with a life cycle added.'),
  ('procurement', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'On the materials tender''s fixture schedule (minWeeks 8, maxWeeks 14, ratePerWeek 0.0025), a bid''s delivery is stated as 15 weeks. What does the evaluation do with it?', '["The bid is excluded at the commercial stage with its reason, since beyond maxWeeks a bid is rejected", "An adjustment of 0.0025 x 7 of the price is added and the bid is kept in the ranking as a late one", "A cap at 14 weeks on the adjustment, with the bid ranked on that capped figure among the responsive bids", "It refuses the whole call, since a completion beyond maxWeeks is an input error"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s schedule basis ends with the maximum: on the well services tender it reads "beyond 10 weeks the bid is rejected", and the materials tender states maxWeeks 14. A bid beyond the maximum leaves the commercial envelope as an exclusion with its reason, which the engine words for a 15-week bid as "offers completion in 15 weeks, beyond the maximum 14 weeks; the bid is nonresponsive", and the adjustment applies only to the weeks beyond minWeeks up to the maximum. A late bid is a result with a reason, so nothing is refused.');
